    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d942">

                <pb facs="0900" n="586" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg586"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111G27/6</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kato</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Japanese
                        Embassy at Washington</hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-10-30">undated</date> [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Received October 30, 1914</hi>.<lb/>] [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Left at the Department of State <lb/> by the Japanese
                        Ambassador <lb/> October 31, 4:15 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Substance of telegram]</p>
                <p>I am advised that several members of the complement of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Geier</hi> are under suspicion of having left Honolulu
                    for San Francisco on October 27 by the S. S. <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Korea</hi>.</p>
                <p>In the above connection, it must be noted that there is nothing to prevent them
                    from conveying intelligence relating to the military matters of Japan, which
                    would be irreconcilable with the principle of the United States Government,
                    actually prescribing limitations on communication between the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hizen</hi> and the land as well as on use of the
                    wireless, etc.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d942-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Moreover, in case the <hi rendition="#italic">Geier</hi> will have eventually
                    been interned, the question concerning the disposition of the complement of the
                    vessel naturally arises. Having this eventuality in view, it is deemed to be an
                    obligation on the part of the United States, as a neutral, to prevent, in the
                    meantime, any member of the complement from leaving the vessel for any distant
                    point.</p>
                <p>Consequently, should the above advice prove to be correct, it is considered
                    proper that steps should be taken to cause these persons to return immediately
                    to their vessel.</p>
                <pb facs="0901" n="587" xml:id="pg587"/>
                <p rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</p>
                <p>Members of the complement of the <hi rendition="#italic">Geier</hi> who are under
                    suspicion of having left Honolulu for San Francisco on October 27: <table
                        cols="2" rows="3">
                        <row role="label">
                            <cell>As first-class passengers</cell>
                            <cell>As second-class passengers</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Egon Pretzel</cell>
                            <cell>Fred Pahrish</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Walter Sourbeck</cell>
                            <cell>Paul Streibel</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
